    "problem_statement": "### Issue: AttributeError when rendering tables with custom styles\n\n#### Describe the bug\n\nAfter updating the `field_names` setter in the `PrettyTable` class, an `AttributeError` is raised when attempting to render tables with custom styles. This issue seems to occur when using specific table styles, such as `ColorTable` with themes or when setting custom alignment and formatting options.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n1. Create a `PrettyTable` instance with custom field names.\n2. Set a custom style or theme for the table.\n3. Attempt to render the table using the `get_string()` or `get_html_string()` methods.\n\nExample code to reproduce the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable, TableStyle\n\n# Create a table with custom field names\ntable = PrettyTable(['A', 'B', 'C'])\n\n# Set a custom style\ntable.set_style(TableStyle.MARKDOWN)\n\n# Add rows to the table\ntable.add_row([1, 2, 3])\ntable.add_row([4, 5, 6])\n\n# Attempt to render the table\nprint(table.get_string())\n```\n\n#### Expected Results\n\nThe table should render correctly with the specified style, displaying all rows and columns without errors.\n\n#### Actual Results\n\nAn `AttributeError` is raised, indicating that certain attributes related to alignment or formatting are missing or not set correctly.\n\n#### Additional Information\n\nThis issue seems to affect various rendering methods and styles, including HTML and color-themed tables. It may be related to the recent changes in how field names and their associated properties are managed within the `PrettyTable` class."
